The default unittest runner can run a selection of unittests

`--DRT-testfilter` restricts which unittests the default runner executes. It
takes a comma-separated list of names of packages, modules or unittests, where
a unittest is named by its module followed by its compiler-generated name:

```
./app --DRT-testfilter=my.pkg                        # every unittest in package my.pkg
./app --DRT-testfilter=my.pkg.mod,other.mod          # the unittests of two modules
./app --DRT-testfilter=my.pkg.mod.__unittest_L9_C1   # a single unittest
./app --DRT-testfilter=my.pkg,-my.pkg.slow           # my.pkg without my.pkg.slow
```

Names match whole components, so `my.pkg` does not select `my.pkgs.mod`. A
name prefixed with `-` excludes what it names, and a list of only such names
runs everything else.

A name that does not match any unittest is an error, as is a selection that
ends up empty, and no unittest is run in either case. `--DRT-testfilter` cannot
be used together with `--DRT-testmode=run-main`, `test-only` or `test-or-main`,
which run unittests per module.

`--DRT-testmode=list` prints the names of the unittests on `stdout`, one per
line, without running them or `main`. Combined with `--DRT-testfilter` it only
prints the selected ones.

An unknown `--DRT-testmode` value is now reported as an error before any
unittest is run.

Custom handlers set with $(D Runtime.moduleUnitTester) or
$(D Runtime.extendedModuleUnitTester) are not affected by either option.
